WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.864
Each payment holds a merchant id.

2
00:00:04.864 --> 00:00:13.060
To show the merchant's name, the database joins the two tables: it pairs each payment with the merchant that has the same id.

3
00:00:13.267 --> 00:00:15.729
The simplest method is a nested loop.

4
00:00:15.729 --> 00:00:21.387
Take payment one and compare it with every merchant: four comparisons, and one match.

5
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.401
Then payment two against all four merchants, and so on down the list.

6
00:00:26.401 --> 00:00:30.019
Six payments times four merchants is 24 comparisons.

7
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.859
A hash join avoids most of that work.

8
00:00:32.859 --> 00:00:40.568
First it reads the smaller table, merchants, and builds a hash table in memory, with one bucket for each hash value.

9
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.607
The hash here is the id mod four, the remainder after dividing by four.

10
00:00:45.607 --> 00:00:47.977
Merchant four lands in bucket zero.

11
00:00:47.977 --> 00:00:51.295
That is the build phase: four inserts, done once.

12
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:54.271
Then the probe phase reads each payment once.

13
00:00:54.271 --> 00:00:56.056
Payment one has merchant one.

14
00:00:56.056 --> 00:01:00.736
One mod four is one, so it goes straight to bucket one, and finds its match.

15
00:01:00.967 --> 00:01:04.657
Payment five has merchant five, which isn't in this table.

16
00:01:04.657 --> 00:01:08.728
Its hash is also one, but the key in bucket one is merchant one.

17
00:01:08.728 --> 00:01:11.909
No match, so an inner join outputs nothing for it.

18
00:01:12.133 --> 00:01:17.623
The other payments work the same way: one hash, one bucket and one comparison each.

19
00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:21.633
In total, four inserts and six probes: ten steps.

20
00:01:21.633 --> 00:01:23.649
The nested loop needed 24.

21
00:01:23.867 --> 00:01:25.844
The gap grows with the data.

22
00:01:25.844 --> 00:01:32.975
For a million payments and 2,500 merchants, the nested loop needs two and a half billion comparisons.

23
00:01:32.975 --> 00:01:35.940
The hash join needs about a million steps.

24
00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:40.480
In the lesson's test, the hash join took 174 milliseconds.

25
00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:43.455
Brute force would take about 40 seconds.

26
00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:49.537
So build a hash table once from the smaller table, then read the bigger table once and probe it.

27
00:01:49.537 --> 00:01:54.368
This works only for an equals condition, and the hash table must fit in memory.
